An English learner (about CEFR B1–B2) revised an email after feedback. For each earlier point, decide whether the ORIGINAL PROBLEM is still in the REVISED email.
- "resolved": the problem is gone. The learner may have fixed it in their own way; they do NOT need to use any particular wording.
- "still_present": the same problem is still there (possibly in changed words).
- "unclear": you cannot tell, or the revision makes the meaning different so the point no longer applies clearly.
Judge only the listed problem. Do not look for new problems here and do not reward or punish copying.
Read the WHOLE revised email. An unchanged phrase may now be correct because its context, referent,
or explanation changed. Never infer that the problem remains merely because the old words remain.
Do not infer the learner's intended meaning when it is ambiguous; choose "unclear" instead.
Return every listed id exactly once. A missing answer is a failed check, not an uncertain judgement.
"current_quote": copy exactly from the REVISED email the words that now carry this part of the message. Leave it empty only if the learner removed that part.
"note": one short sentence in simple English.

EARLIER EMAIL:
"""{{ old_text }}"""

REVISED EMAIL:
"""{{ new_text }}"""

EARLIER POINTS:
{% for item in items %}- id: {{ item.id }} | type: {{ item.type }}
  the learner had written: """{{ item.quote }}"""
  problem: {{ item.reason }}
{% endfor %}
Output ONLY JSON: {"results": [{"id": "...", "status": "resolved|still_present|unclear", "current_quote": "...", "note": "..."}]}
